Certainly! Let's go through examples of modal dialogs with a focus on bounce animations. I'll provide both an advertisement and a non-advertisement example.

### Advertisement Example:
Here's a modal dialog that could be used for a promotional ad on a website. It uses bounce animations to catch the user's attention.

```html
<div class="modal ad-modal" id="promo-modal" data-ad-id="promo123" data-campaign="spring-offers">
 <style>
 .ad-modal {
 display: none;
 position: fixed;
 top: 50%;
 left: 50%;
 transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
 width: 300px;
 background-color: white;
 box-shadow: 0 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
 border-radius: 8px;
 animation: bounceIn 1s ease;
 }
 .modal-content {
 padding: 20px;
 text-align: center;
 }
 @keyframes bounceIn {
 0%, 20%, 50%, 80%, 100% {
 transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(1.1);
 }
 40% {
 transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(1.0);
 }
 60% {
 transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(1.05);
 }
 }
 </style>
 <div class="modal-content">
 <h2>Special Offer!</h2>
 <p>Subscribe now and get 50% off your first purchase!</p>
 <button onclick="hideModal()">Close</button>
 </div>
 <script>
 function showModal() {
 document.getElementById('promo-modal').style.display = 'block';
 }
 function hideModal() {
 document.getElementById('promo-modal').style.display = 'none';
 }
 window.onload = showModal;
 </script>
</div>
```
Classification: is_ad: true
Ad Type: modal

### Non-Advertisement Example:
